  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The performance and trade-offs of the model are demonstrated using simulated benchmark instances rather than real-world distribution data.",
      "what": "This study introduces a robust optimization model and an adaptive large neighborhood search (ALNS) algorithm to solve the vehicle routing problem with cross-docking for perishable products under travel time and freshness-life uncertainties. Tested on benchmark instances with up to 200 requests, the ALNS algorithm maintains stable performance across 10 runs, while sensitivity analysis shows that accounting for freshness constraints reduces freshness loss by 47.12% at an average total cost increase of 9.61% for robustness.",
      "why": "It provides logistics planners with a computationally viable method to balance the trade-offs between delivery costs, product quality, and schedule robustness when distributing highly perishable goods through cross-docking networks."
    }
  },
